\section{Reading the measurements}\label{sec:read}

\paragraph{Recovering the correlation.}
At $u=2.5$ the baseline achieves $R^{2}_{\text{before}}=0.3927$ and the augmented model
$0.5691$, so $\Delta R^{2}=0.1764$. By Theorem~\ref{thm:corr},
$\rho^{2}=0.1764/0.6073=0.2905$, i.e.\ $|\rho|\approx0.539$: the unexplained component of
the yield was correlated with the footprint dial at roughly $0.54$.

\paragraph{The ceiling is not binding.}
Corollary~\ref{cor:ceiling} caps the lift at $0.6073$; the observed $0.176$ uses $29\%$ of
the headroom. At $u=3.5$, $R^{2}$ moves $0.2063\to0.3078$, so $\Delta R^{2}=0.1015$ against
a ceiling of $0.7937$, giving $|\rho|\approx0.358$.

\paragraph{Non-degeneracy.}
Corollary~\ref{cor:nondeg} rules out the failure mode of a near-constant regressor. For
$B=400$ (the $77$ odd primes up to $400$) the variance is $\approx0.18740$, standard
deviation $\approx0.43289$ against a mean $\approx1.76067$: a coefficient of variation near
$24.6\%$.

\paragraph{Cost.}
Evaluating $W(N,B)$ at $B=400$ costs $77$ Euler-criterion tests plus a rational
accumulation --- negligible against the sieve itself.

\section{Applications}\label{sec:apps}

\paragraph{Parameter calibration.} A per-modulus predictor of sieve yield, computable in
microseconds, whose distribution is known exactly (Theorem~\ref{thm:law}), so calibration
curves can be normalised analytically rather than by simulation.

\paragraph{Benchmark design.} Theorem~\ref{thm:range} lets one \emph{construct} moduli with
a prescribed footprint, testing an implementation across the full dynamic range of
arithmetic difficulty rather than sampling and hoping.

\paragraph{Guarantees for learned heuristics.} Theorems~\ref{thm:cap} and~\ref{thm:blind}
form a template: a feature invariant under congruence modulo a fixed modulus inherits, via
Dirichlet, a proof that it leaks no factorization information.

\paragraph{Sieve accounting with prime powers.} Corollary~\ref{cor:ppdens} licenses
charging a prime power $p^{k}$ a weight proportional to the exact density $2/p^{k}$;
Theorem~\ref{thm:mod8} explains why the even prime must be special-cased.

\section{Discussion and future directions}\label{sec:future}

The shape of the work is a pair of complementary exactness results: an informal density
slogan upgraded to an identity, an empirical lift upgraded to a measured correlation, a
vague ``the feature varies'' upgraded to a closed-form variance and exact capacity --- and,
on the negative side, an informal barrier upgraded to an unconditional theorem.

\begin{description}
\item[D1. Limit law of the centred dial.] Since the variances $(p^{2}-1)/p^{4}$ are
summable while the terms are bounded, the centred dial is a weighted Bernoulli convolution
over the odd primes with success probabilities $(p+1)/(2p)$ and weights $2/p$. Identify its
limit law: absolute continuity, tail bounds, Berry--Esseen constants in $B$.
\item[D2. Higher moments and concentration.] The exact independence gives the moment
generating function $\prod_p\bigl(\frac{p-1}{2p}+\frac{p+1}{2p}e^{2t/p}\bigr)$; the
resulting Chernoff bounds would yield explicit prediction intervals for the yield model.
\item[D3. Higher-degree generalisations.] For the number field sieve the local count is the
number of roots of a degree-$d$ polynomial mod $p$, governed by splitting type. The
mean-footprint identity should hold verbatim, with Chebotarev replacing
Lemma~\ref{lem:local} --- and, presumably, an analogous blindness theorem.
\item[D4. Prime powers in the dial.] Corollary~\ref{cor:ppdens} supplies exact prime-power
densities; adding the correction $2/(p(p-1))-2/p$ refines the dial by $O(\sum1/p^{2})$, of
the same order as the variance. Is the refinement detectable?
\item[D5. Two-adic structure.] Theorem~\ref{thm:mod8} is the vanishing half; for
$N\equiv1\pmod 8$ one has $h(N,2^{k})=4$ for $k\ge3$, a large bimodal term suggesting a
binary feature $\mathbf 1[N\equiv1\bmod 8]$ with heavy weight.
\item[D6. Interaction with the direct mechanism feature.] Theorem~\ref{thm:lift} predicts
the joint lift once the mutual correlation of the two features is known; both are functions
of the same residues, so that correlation should be computable in closed form.
\item[D7. Sharpening the barrier.] Plausibly, for every fixed factorization shape, every
dial value is attained by arbitrarily large integers of that shape --- blindness to the
entire factorization type, not merely to a binary distinction.
\end{description}

\section{Conclusion}

The residual of a quadratic-sieve yield model is not noise; it is the arithmetic of
quadratic residues, captured by a single number costing a few dozen modular
exponentiations. That number is exactly the mean sieve footprint over a period, exactly
independent across primes, has exactly computable mean and variance, attains exactly
$2^{|\base(B)|}$ values, and lifts the coefficient of determination by exactly the squared
residual correlation. It is also, provably and unconditionally, incapable of saying
anything about the factorization of its input. Both halves matter: a practical calibration
problem has an exact arithmetic solution, and that solution is provably a description of
the method rather than a window into the secret.

